t a few minutes, he said\u2026\"\n[1272] \"Which book?\" Roeve furrowed his brow. \"Klaber, you never told me about this!\" [1273] \"Child, did I not? I must have forgotten.\u201d Klaber chuckled. \"It was the 1599 edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' by Jaggard!\" [1274] They fell silent for a moment, none daring to look at the others. [1275] \"Go on,\" Lane whispered, urging him. \"So you just handed him the book?\" [1276] Clarber smiled ugly. \"Clarber would never! I said, sir, no. I said the rules didn't allow it. He nodded, not seeming surprised, and looked around again. I began to doubt, but he went on about the book... Finally, he walked back to the desk, where there were some papers - note cards and envelopes. His eyes flickered with a strange expression, and then he said, 'Is this the stationery of the Saxon Library, Mr. Clarber?' I said, 'Yes.' He looked pitifully at me and said, 'Ha! Ha! Very interesting. It's hard to get into this place. Do you know, I made a bet with a friend that I could get in? Look, good sir, I did it!' I replied, 'Oh, you did, didn't you?' He said, 'Ha! I'm really here now. Will you do me a favor, old man, and let me prove I won the bet? I need to prove I came. Ah, yes.' He seemed to have just thought of it, picking up a note card and an envelope. 'This proves it. Mr. Clarber, thank you a thousand times!' Before I could say anything, he ran out.\" [1277] The inspector listened in astonishment to this extraordinary story, but as soon as Clarber's lips closed, he roared, \"A bunch of idiots! You let him go? Why -\"\n[1278] \"That's how we got the note,\" Peachins said slowly. [1279] \"Dear,\" Ren's voice was low. \"Unless necessary, let's not waste Clarber's time anymore. Clarber, can you describe this peculiar guest?\" [1280] \"Of course. Tall, slender, middle-aged, very British-looking.\" [1281] \"Good heavens!\" the inspector's voice was hoarse. \"Pett, this is\u2014\"\n[1282] \"Inspector, please.\n <Assistant> I have read the book 