nce is enough to make one believe...,\" his voice trailed off. He did not move his gaze from the inspector's lips, but it seemed his eyes did not see anything, for there was a glow over them, like a veil had fallen\u2014a veil that hid the sudden insight that had entered his eyes. [1094] \"But I don't understand...,\" Percy said, bewildered. [1095] Reine shuddered, the veil disappeared. \"Inspector, open it!\" he said, leaning forward, his hands resting on his chin. [1096] Sam picked up the letter opener again and inserted the blade into the seal, slowly applying pressure. The paper was stiff and reluctant to yield. [1097] Raine and Percy did not blink an eye. [1098] Sam's thick fingers reached into the envelope and pulled out a sheet of pale gray paper, the same tone as the envelope, neatly folded. He unfolded the paper. There was some text printed at one end. The inspector turned the paper over, and the text on the other side was simple: Saxon Library. The text was gray, a darker shade than the paper. He laid the paper flat on the pad, placing it between himself and Raine. They both stared at the paper, and the office fell into silence. [1099] The reason was: if the imposter was a mysterious figure, the secret he held in the inspector's hand was even more mysterious - not only mysterious, but also eerie. There was no logic in this at all. [1100] The letter had \"Saxon Library\" printed at the top, and the rest of the page was as smooth as freshly printed paper, with only a few words, or symbols, written near the center, just below the printed heading. [1101] Nothing more. No decipherable information, no signature, no mark made by pen or pencil. [1102] The inspector's old body suddenly trembled with excitement, hunched over in his chair, eyes fixed on the characters on the paper. The paper slipped from his fingers. Percy remained motionless. For a long time, no one moved. Eventually, the old man slowly shifted his gaze from the paper to Sam. In the depths of his clear eyes, there was a hint of triumph, almost a gleam of euphoria. [1103] However, the inspector muttered, \"3HS wM.\" His tone was bewildered, the syllables rolling off his tongue, as if he were trying to unravel the meaning hidden within the sounds. [1104] Ren's face showed a touch of confusion, glancing quickly at Percy. [1105] She murmured, \"3HS wM,\" as if a child repeating foreign language. [1106] The old man buried his face in his hands and sat there motionless. [1107] * * *\n[1108] \"Well!\" The inspector finally sighed deeply, \"I give up, damn it, I give up. A guy walks in dressed like a clown in a parade and leaves a bunch of crazy talk about 'a million-dollar secret' - tell you, I give up. It's a joke, someone's joke.\" [1109] Pechin swiftly circled around her father's chair and picked up the paper. She furrowed her brow and concentrated on deciphering the meaning of the symbols. The inspector pushed his chair back to the window and watched the bustling Times Square outside. [1110] Philo Rine suddenly looked up and quietly asked, \"Pechin, let me take a look, please?\" [1111] Pechin sat down, bewildered, as the old man took the note from her hand and examined the cryptic text. [1112] The symbol was hastily written, resembling calligraphy, and ink that was likely the darkest. The fluidity and confidence of the strokes indicated no hesitation, as the writer clearly knew what he was writing and executed it with one stroke. [1113] Ren put down the note, picked up the gray envelope and examined it, flipping it over and over again. The words \"Saxon Library\" printed on the seal seemed to fascinate him. He felt the seal with his fingertips, the black letters printed on it shining brightly, touching his fingertips' sensory nerves. [1114] He put down the envelope, closed his eyes, and leaned back, murmuring, \"Sergeant, this is not a joke.\" [1115] Sam slid his chair. \"So what the hell does it mean? If it's not a joke, it must mean something... damn it, he said it was just a 'clue.' He wasn't wrong, it's the most confusing clue I've ever seen. Intentionally complicated, huh? Hmmph!\" He went back to the window. [1116]Percy furrowed his brow again. \"It can't be that hard to understand. He probably wanted to make it mysterious. He should have made it simpler so we could figure it out after careful study. Let me see... maybe it's some kind of abbreviation, right? Containing some information?\" [1117]The inspector sighed without turning around. [1118]\"Or,\" Percy continued to ponder, \"it could be chemical symbols. H for hydrogen, right? And S for sulfur. Hydrogen-sulfur. Right!\u201d\n[1119]\"No,\" Reynolds said in a low voice, \"I think the molecular formula for hydrogen sulfide is H2S. It seems that HS has nothing to do with chemistry, Percy.\" [1120]\"There's still...\" Percy said dejectedly, \"the small w and the capital M... Oh, God, it's confusing. I wish Gordon were here; he knows a lot of useless things.\" [1121] The sergeant slowly turned around. \"Of course, no headway.\u201d His tone was odd, \"For us, that is, Petty; for your lively Mr. Rowe, too. But don't forget, this mysterious person involves Reynolds, so he may think Reynolds would know what it means\u2026 Hey, Reynolds?\u201d\n[1122] Ren remained indifferent to this obvious challenge, and then wrinkles appeared at the corners of his eyes. \"Suspect me? Maybe it is as you think, old man.\" [1123] \"Oh, so what the hell does that mean?\" the officer asked bluntly, walking over. [1124] Ren waved his fair hand and continued to stare at the paper in front of him, muttering, \"It's strange, I believe he thought you would also know what this means.\" [1125] The sergeant flushed and stood up, walking towards the door. \"Miss Brodie! Bring in the ledger.\" [1126] Miss Broadie quickly entered, holding a pencil. [1127] \"Write a letter to Dr. Leo Shilling of the Laboratory. 'Dear Doctor: Please attend to this matter immediately. Do not disclose. Does the following 