orning, and he \u2014 he didn't mention anything about it. I believe \u2014\"\n[1150] \"Listen, Petty,\" the inspector's tone had a warning note. \"Just listen to your father once! Petty, you're interested in that boy, aren't you? Is he pursuing you? Humph, I'll break his neck!\" [1151] Percy stood up, saying angrily, \"Dad!\" [1152] \"All right, all right, Sergeant,\" the old man murmured, \"don't be so stubborn. Gordon Lowe is a fine young man, and his scholarship matches yours, which is rare!\" [1153] \"I tell you, I don't love him!\" Percy called, \"Dad, you're terrible. Can't I be good to a man?\" [1154] The sergeant looked pitiful. [1155] Mr. Jules Reine stood up. \"Stop arguing. Sergeant, you're so childish. Put this paper and envelope back in the safe. We have to go to the Saxon's house.\" [1156] Chapter 12\n[1157] Good luck to me\n[1158] Traffic was heavy, and the Lincoln crawled along Fifth Avenue, Dromio sweating with impatience. But Mr. Vance seemed unruffled; he glanced at Sam and Paisin and chuckled. \"You two are a pair of bad-tempered kids. Smile!\" [1159] \"You're a pair of bad-tempered kids,\" he continued. \"What an extraordinary case! I don't think you realize how extraordinary it is.\" [1160] \"I have a headache,\" the Chief complained. [1161]\"And you, Peachum?\" [1162]\"I think\u2014\" Peachum was still watching Dromio's red back of the head, \"\u2014that those symbols mean a lot more to you than they do to us.\" [1163]The old gentleman was slightly surprised, and he leaned forward, his eyes sharp as he studied her smooth, young face. \"Perhaps so,\" he said, \"but in the past. Inspector, any progress? There's been so much going on this morning that I haven't had a chance to ask.\" [1164]\"Quite a bit,\" the inspector said wearily, \"Mr. Broude has it all down. I know you'd want to know.\" [1165]Donohue: Still missing, no trace found. [1166] Seventeen teachers: Back in Indiana. All identities checked out without suspicion. Investigation conduc