sed to investigate quietly. Then the sergeant transferred the call to Inspector Grogan. [642] \"Hey, Butcher, it's me again. Have you heard anything about a slippery thief who specializes in stealing rare books? A guy with a somewhat odd blue hat\u2026 I'm not sure, maybe it's just a habit of his.\" [643] \"A book thief, huh?\" Grogan thought for a moment. \"Blue hat\u2026 I can't recall anyone fitting that description. I'll check and call you back.\" [644] \"Thank you, I'll wait for your call.\" [645] Half an hour later, Grogan called back. There was no such thief in the criminal records of the police department: one who specialized in stealing books and had the habit of wearing blue or light blue hats. [646] The inspector looked out the window, puzzled by the dull world. He finally sighed, pulled out a sheet of paper from his desk, opened the pen cap, and began to write a letter with care:\n[647] Dear Lane,\n[648] There are some things that may interest you, which I mentioned to Quincy this morning on the phone. To be honest, both Petty and I are at a loss and need your advice. A retired policeman named Donohue...\n[649] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[650] (1) John Milton (1608-1674), an English poet, wrote \"Paradise Lost.\" [651] (2) Stratford, the hometown of Shakespeare. [652] Chapter 7\n[653] \"The Fervent Pilgrim\"\n[654] Miss Broady stumbled into the boss's office, her young, stiff face full of excitement. \"Oh, Sergeant! It's... it's Rayne, sir!\" [655] \"What is it?\" the sergeant asked calmly - it was Tuesday, and he had already forgotten writing a letter to Rayne the day before. [656] \"Well, well, Miss Brody,\" Perkins said affably, \"don't be flustered, what's the matter with Mr. Lane?\" [657] Miss Brody took a deep breath and pointed shakily at the door. \"He's out there.\" [658] \"Holy smoke, raining cats and dogs!\" the Chief exclaimed, jumping to the door, 