ays. [673] \"I agree.\" Ren says casually. \"A clever young man, isn't he?\" [674] \"Yes, yes.\" [675] The inspector is somewhat agitated. \"Ren, actually, what we're dealing with is quite mad. I haven't taken any fees. This is the craziest thing you've ever heard of. For old times' sake, I have to do something about it.\" [676] \"Not worth envying,\" the old gentleman couldn't help murmuring, \"I think we should go to the museum right away. Inspector, you described the broken glass cabinet in the Saxon room, and I'd like to check out what's inside.\" [677] \"Oh!\" Pechin exclaimed, \"Did I miss something?\" [678] \"Just a guess,\" Mr. Lerne said thoughtfully. \"I'm sure there's nothing. Let's go, Dromio is waiting for us downstairs.\" [679] They found Dr. Allonsio Jotte talking to a tall, thin man with long limbs and wearing strange foreign clothes. He had a typical British narrow face and sharp eyes, a brown, unframed spectacles perched on the bridge of his nose, a black silk ribbon attached to the frame, which wrapped around his neck. His bony face was clean-shaven, and his well-groomed beard gave him the appearance of a Renaissance scholar. His tone was calm and confident, and his refined accent revealed that he was an educated Englishman. He appeared to be around fifty years old. Dr. Jotte introduced him as Dr. Hamnett Sedra, the future curator, who had arrived on a British ship that morning. [680] \"Mr. Lerne!\" he exclaimed. \"It's an honor to meet you. Ever since I saw your performance of 'Othello' in London twenty years ago, I've wanted to meet you. And then, your scholarly articles on Shakespeare in 'The Review'\u2026\"\n[681] \"You're too kind,\" the old man hastened to say, \"I'm only half-knowledgeable in literature. I believe Dr. Quart told you about the strange incident that occurred here before you arrived?\" [682] Dr. Sedley seemed unaware of the matter. \"I'm sorry, what?\" [683] \"Oh, a trivial matter,\" Dr. Quart reluctantly said, stroking his goat's beard, \"Mr. Lenoir, I'm surprised you take this so seriously.\" [684] \"The circumstances seem quite odd and superficial, Dr. Sedley,\" Mr. Lenoir whispered, his bright eyes darting from Dr. Quart to Dr. Sedley and back again, \"Dr. Sedley, on Monday\u2014two days ago, someone obviously infiltrated the museum and clearly disturbed a cabinet in this new room.\" [685] \"Really?\" Dr. Sedley asked. [686] \"Nothing serious.\" The curator said impatiently, \"He didn't take anything, and that's the most important thing.\" [687] \"I suppose so.\" The British man smiled in agreement. [688] \"Can I interrupt this academic dispute?\" The old gentleman said, \"Can I suggest re-examining the evidence itself? Or would you all rather...\"\n[689] Dr. Jotter nodded. The British man said, \"I believe I'm quite familiar with Dr. Jotter by now. I have nothing more important to do than to look at this broken glass cabinet.\" [690] \"Oh, yes, yes.\" The curator furrowed his brow, \"Since you want to see it, of course, you can.\" [691] They walked through the empty general reading room. Percyns glanced around, a little disappointed: where was Gordon Roeve? They entered the Saxon room. The glass that had been broken yesterday had been replaced, and the cabinet was as spotless and gleaming as the rest of the room. [692] \"The glazier fixed it yesterday,\" Dr. Jott challenged the sergeant, \"I can assure you he was never alone. I stood right next to him until he finished the job.\" [693] The sergeant snorted. [694] Mr. Lane and Dr. Sedra looked at the glass curiously, their eyes shining with admiration. [695] \"Jagger's.\" Dr. Sedra said gently, \"Mr. Lane, that's remarkable. Dr. Jott, you say this room is new, and these artifacts are recent donations?\" [696] \"Yes. The collections in this room were bequeathed to Britain by the collector Samuel Saxon. When the museum reopens, they will of course be on display.\" [697] \"Oh, of course. I remember Mr. Veses mentioning this to me a month ago in London. I often wonder what Mr. Saxon's library in America contains. Quite mysterious, isn't it? These Jagger collections - they're too beautiful.\" [698] Mr. Reane looked intently at the collections in the glass. \"Dr. Jotter, do you have the key to this cabinet?\" [699] \"Of course.\" [700] \"Would you mind opening it for me?\" [701] The curator looked surprised for a moment, but eventually complied with the request. The elderly gentleman opened the glass lid, put it back in place, and everyone gathered around. Three ancient books lay naked on the soft black velvet. Under the intense light at the top of the cabinet, the faded colors came to life, capturing everyone's attention. Mr. Reane carefully picked up each of the leather-bound books, examining them with great care, checking the covers, bindings, endpapers, and contents... For a moment, he checked the contents again. He put the three books back in their original position, stood up straight, and his chiseled features suddenly became serious. [702] \"It's very strange,\" he murmured. \"I don't believe it.\" He looked at the open cabinet again. [703] \"What's going on?\" Dr. Jowett asked in a low voice. [704] \"This, my dear Jowett -\" the elderly gentleman said calmly - \"one of the books that was originally here has been stolen!\" [705] \"Stolen!\" they exclaimed simultaneously. Dr. Jowett took a step forward and stopped. [706] \"Impossible!\" he exclaimed. \"I personally checked these Jagger's when Rowe discovered the cabinet's glass was broken.\" [707] \"Did you check the inside?\" Raine asked quietly. [708] The librarian's face paled. \"I didn't see... No. But the most cursory check...\"\n[709] \"\u2026probably deceived your trained eyes, doctor. I said it was the strangest thing in my experience.\" He raised his thin, silver eyebrows, \"Look here!\" His long, thin fingers pointed to a triangular card placed between the three books, the blue leather-bound book in the middle. The card read:\n[710] Eager Pilgrim\n[711] William Shakespeare\n[712] (J\u00e4ger, 1599)\n[713] This is a precious and unique collection from Samuel Saxon Library. The first edition of this book has only three known copies in existence, and this is one of them, published in 1599 by William J\u00e4ger, a notorious printer of Elizabethan times. J\u00e4ger claimed it was Shakespeare's work, but only five of the twenty poems are attributed to Shakespeare, the rest are attributed to Richard Brome (1), Bartholomew Griffin (2), and other contemporary poets. [714] \"What is it?\" Dr. Jotter asked quietly. Dr. Sidel's eyes gleamed through his monocle at the middle book, but it seemed he didn't see the card behind it. [715] \"This... this is a forgery, isn't it?\" Percy gasped. [716] \"No, dear Percy. I wouldn't dare claim to be an expert, but I can confidently say that you are looking at a genuine Jagger edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim.'\" [717] Dr. Jotter was angry. \"I don't see \u2013\" He picked up the blue leather-bound book and flipped to the title page, his jaw dropping in shock. Dr. Siddra looked over his shoulder in surprise and displayed a look of utter shock. [718] Reine paced back and forth behind the cabinet, head down. [719] \"Well, but \u2013\" The sergeant didn't know how to start. He shook his hands and muttered a string of curses. [720] \"If it's a genuine Jagger,\" Percy said loudly, \"how \u2013\"\n[721] \"Completely, absolutely impossible, impossible,\" Dr. Jotter muttered over and over again. [722] \"Bloody hell,\" the British man said in awe. [723] The two men bent over the book, eagerly flipping through the pages. They looked at each other, nodding with some respect, and then returned their attention to the book's first page. Percy watched from behind them, and the page read:\n[724] \"The Pilgrim of Love, also known as the sonnets between Venus and Adonis. Written by Shakespeare. Second edition, printed by William Jaggard, 1606.\" [725] \"I see,\" Percy said slowly. \"This is not the 1599 first edition of Jaggard, but the 1606 Jaggard, or the second edition. Obviously, it's worth less...\"\n[726] \"My dear Miss Sam,\u201d Dr. Jotter spoke without turning around, \"you are quite mistaken.\" [727] \"You mean it's more valuable?\" [728] The constable's interest was revived. Reine continued to pace on the floor, lost in thought. [729] No one answered, and Pechin blushed and stepped aside. [730] \"Pechin!\" The old gentleman called out to her, and she gratefully went over to him. He put his arm around her shoulder, \"My dear Pechin, do you know what makes this event so extraordinary?\" [731] \"I couldn't possibly imagine.\" [732] Raine gently squeezed her shoulder. \"William Jagger was a keen supporter of the arts. In the days when Shakespeare, Jonson, Fletcher (3), Marlowe, and others were making fortunes, he was there too. Publishers might have competed fiercely, and William Jagger sought to capitalize on the fame, just as some drama producers and book publishers do today. So, he became a kind of pirate, printing 'The Passionate Pilgrim,' which collected two sonnets by Shakespeare that were not previously published, and three sonnets from the already published play 'Much Ado About Nothing,' with the rest being filler. He audaciously claimed that all the poems were written by Shakespeare. I have no doubt they sold well. As for Shakespeare himself, as a playwright, he was surprisingly indifferent to publishing matters.\" [733] \"So, this book is priceless?\" [734] \"Priceless?\" Dr. Jotter responded blankly. [735] \"I mean...,\" the old man's voice was pleasant, \"This is a very unusual case. Inspector, I don't blame you for being confused. You haven't yet figured out the whole mystery. Dear little Pethurst, the situation has become quite mad. Clearly, the man in the blue cap has gone through great trouble and taken great risks to infiltrate a closed circle, sneak into the British Museum, and break into the Saxon room, smashing the glas