nuine 1599 Jagger, he didn't even touch the fake one and returned it.\" [956] \"So, he's probably a collector or something like that, right?\" Percy whispered. [957] \"Yes, dear, that's a very good analysis,\" the old man said, getting up and pacing around the room. \"We've put together a very enlightening picture. A scholar, an antiquarian, a collector, fundamentally honest, who went to great lengths to commit theft in order to obtain\u2014I'm sure\u2014a paper hidden inside an old and valuable book. Interesting, isn't it?\" [958] Sam interjected, \"I wonder what he could gain from it?\" [959] \"The opening, or the recess,\" Roe thought for a moment, \"is about five inches long and three inches wide. If it's a piece of paper, it might be folded and could be very old.\" [960] \"I think so,\" Raine murmured. \"Although perhaps not. Well, things are quite clear now. I think I should do some small investigations of my own.\" [961] \"About Donoho?\" The sergeant asked hopefully. [962] Lain smiled. \"No. That's your job; you'll do it better than I would. I'm thinking of...,\" he continued, frowning, \"a little research. You know, I have a rather remarkable library of my own.\" [963] \"It's a scholar's paradise,\" Roe said enviously. [964] \"What kind of research?\" Pechinsk asked. [965] \"Dear, check if the damaged Jagger's leather binding is the original. If that doesn't help, it can at least clarify some things. The leather binding of this book could provide clues to determine the year the hidden item was placed there. As Gordon said, it could be some folded document.\" [966] \"Mr. Raine, perhaps I can assist,\" the young man said eagerly. [967] \"Ah!\" The old gentleman said, \"Gordon, that's a good idea. You can work independently, and we can compare our findings.\" [968] \"I agree,\" Percy said, inexplicably happy, \"If there's a document hidden in such an old book, it could be a record. But how did the thief know, and where to look?\" [969] \"Astute observations! I