eant, you have to accept these things. I said Clurman was a slippery character, trying to cover his tracks, but there are some strange things going on that need looking into.\" [986] \"Well, anyway,\" the sergeant grumbled, \"I still don't like... huh? I didn't think of that. If so, I think we should ask Klabber a few questions.\" [987] Pencils examined the cigarette butt, his voice low: \"Dad, you know, this makes me think. Why don't we investigate Dr. Sedlar first instead of bothering Mr. Klabber?\" [988] \"Patti, you mean Britain?\" [989] \"Let's not be greedy. How about the shipping company?\" [990] \"Shipping company? What's that supposed to mean?\" [991] \"Just to see if there's any drama,\" Peachins murmured. [992] Forty-five minutes later, the sergeant put down the phone, wiping his brow with a trembling handkerchief. He finally sighed: \"Well, the show's about to start. It's too absurd... You know what the shipping company's manager just told me?\" [993] \"Oh, Dad,\" Peachins said, \"you're teasing us. What did he say?\" [994] \"There's no Hamnet Sedra on the passenger list!\" [995] They all looked at each other in surprise. Then Gordon Rowe whistled, putting out his cigarette in the sergeant's ashtray. \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"The famous Dr. Sedra...\"\n[996] \"Good, I like it,\" Percy murmured. \"I like it a lot.\" [997] \"Good heavens, he's a fake!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Listen, kid, you can't tell a soul. Not a word, or else I'll\u2014\"\n[998] \"Alright, alright, sergeant,\" Reine interrupted him gently, leaning back on the leather sofa with many wrinkles on his forehead, \"Don't be in such a hurry. A good set-up doesn't necessarily make a good play, and a suspicious situation can't convict someone. Why did you describe Sedra to the secretary?\" [999] \"Well,\" Sam snorted, \"he checked the passenger list and couldn't find the guy's name. I described Sedra to the secretary and asked him to check with the ship's staff. The ship docked this morning, 