ture, haven't you?\" [1027] \"Of course.\" [1028] \"Have you studied Elizabethan literature?\" [1029] Percy's cheeks flushed. \"That\u2014that was a long time ago.\" [1030] The old gentleman shook his head dejectedly. \"Typical modern education. So you've never heard of Hamnet Sedley. Gordon, tell them who Hamnet Sedley is!\" [1031] \"Hamnet Sedley\u2014\" Mr. Rowe's voice was a bit stunned, \"one of William Shakespeare's closest friends.\" [1032] \"Shakespeare!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Rayne, are you mistaken? Are you all crazy? What does old Shakespeare have to do with this?\" [1033] \"It has a lot to do with it. I began to think\u2026\" Rayne whispered, \"Right, Gordon, yes. It's just\u2026\" He pondered, shaking his head, \"You must know. Sedley\u2026 Oh my!\" [1034] \"I'm still not quite getting it,\" Percy complained. \"In that regard, I agree with my father. Of course\u2026\"\n[1035] \"This Sedley guy must be a wandering Jew, right?\" the sergeant joked. \"He can't be over three hundred years old, can he?\" He laughed merrily. [1036] \"Ha, ha!\" Mr. Rawlins sighed deeply. [1037] \"I don't mean our friend is Ahasuerus (2),\" Mr. Raine smiled, \"So far, nothing has been so exaggerated. But what I mean is, Dr. Hamnet Sadler - former director of the Kingston Museum in London, future director of the British Museum in New York, English, cultured, a book collector... Oh, Dr. Sadler is a direct descendant of Shakespeare's friend, it's not completely impossible.\" [1038] \"The family from Stratford?\" Percyns pondered. [1039] The old man shrugged, \"We know nothing about them.\" [1040] \"I think -\" Rawlins said slowly, \"The Sadler family comes from Gloucestershire.\" [1041] \"What difference does that make?\" Pecham protested. \"If Dr. Sedgwick is the descendant of Shakespeare's close friend, does the Sedgwick family have anything to do with this copy of the Jagger edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' from 1599, which caused all this commotion?\" [1042] Mr. Reen said quiet