e cellar. The third time he emerged, he wore a satisfied expression, holding in his hands a jumbled mess of iron pieces, rubber, glass, and wires. [2176] \"How's it going?\" Pauling asked. [2177] \"Sir, the evidence is here.\" The explosives expert said, picking up a fragment of a clock-like instrument, \"A time bomb.\" [2178] \"Ah!\" Mr. Philo Vance said. [2179] \"Primitive, homemade. Set to go off at six o'clock with yellow dynamite.\" [2180] Percy, Roe, and Vance all thought the same thing, but Vance was the first to ask: \"When was the bomb placed?\" [2181] \"At six o'clock on Sunday evening - if the bomb went off last night at six o'clock. It's a twenty-four-hour timer.\" [2182] \"Six o'clock on Sunday evening.\" Perkins repeated slowly, \"So it was put on before Maxwell was attacked on Sunday night!\" [2183] \"You're right, Perri.\" Rowe said, \"If the person who put the bomb knew there were documents in the house, his purpose was to destroy the documents, so he knew the stuff was in the house but didn't know exactly where. It's difficult -\"\n[2184] \"The epicenter of the explosion was the cellar.\" The expert spat on the charred rock. [2185] Reine let out another \"Ah.\" [2186] \"The second visitor \u2013 the one who took the documents out of the secret cabinet \u2013\" Perkins looked pointedly at Reen, \"could not have been the one who planted the bomb. It's obvious. The second visitor knew where the documents were; the bomber didn't, as you said, Gordon\u2026\" Perkins' words were interrupted by a coarse roar, coming from the workers digging through the cellar debris. They all turned around. [2187] \"What is it?\" Bowlin called, running over quickly. [2188] Three people were bending over something, their heads almost touching. One of them turned around, pale and trembling. He managed to speak: \"Sir, there's a body here. From the look on his face, he was \u2013 murdered.\" [2189] The young man ran towards the edge of the house foundation, stepping on black