e attack caused the cuts on both the wrist and the case?\" [2211] \"That's the standard answer!\" [2212] \"This is our axe-wielder,\" Roe's eyes blazed with anger, \"or at least the person who used the axe... Doctor, could these cuts have been made by an axe?\" [2213] \"Yes, it couldn't have been a knife. The blade of the weapon must have been wide and had a handle.\" [2214] \"That settles it,\" Pauling said. \"Someone used an axe on this guy, hitting his wrist, smashing his watch, stopping it, and injuring his wrist. And, I think, the killer fed him lead pellets.\" [2215] \"There's one more thing.\" The coroner pulled a small key wrapped in a tissue from his pocket. \"Pauling, your men just brought it over, they found it in a fragment of a pants pocket near the body. Someone has identified it\u2014\"\n[2216] \"Maxwell?\" [2217] \"The caretaker of the house? Yes, Maxwell identified this as the original key to the front door.\" [2218] \"Original!\" The two young men exclaimed in unison. [2219] \"Strange.\" Pauling said. \"Wait a minute!\" He picked up the prosecutor's phone and dialed the headquarters in Tarrytown. He spoke briefly to someone and then hung up the phone, saying, \"Yes. My men told me this is Dr. Alessi's key. The key taken by the masked man who tied Maxwell up in the garage that night was a copy.\" [2220] \"The only original?\" Percy gasped. [2221] \"That's what Maxwell said.\" [2222] \"So, there's nothing to doubt,\" the prosecutor sighed contentedly. \"The body must be that of Dr. Allais.\" [2223] \"Really?\" Reine murmured. [2224] \"Don't you think so?\" [2225] \"Dear sir, a key does not necessarily represent an owner. However, I suppose that logically, it makes sense.\" [2226] \"Well, I'm busy.\" The forensic expert said, \"And there's one more thing. I think you need the deceased's physical features. He's five feet eleven inches tall, has light yellow or golden hair, weighs about one hundred fifty-five pounds, and is between forty-five and fifty-five years old. I couldn't find any other identifying marks.\" [2227] \"Sedra.\" Percy whispered. [2228] \"Bingo.\" Roe impatiently said, \"A British doctor named Sedra was involved in this case, and he disappeared from his New York hotel on Saturday. This description fits him perfectly.\" [2229] \"You don't mean that guy!\" Paul shouted. [2230] \"I do mean him. However, the identification seems a bit vague. This Sedra person is also suspected to be Dr. Alessi\u2014\"\n[2231] \"So that's it!\" Bauer exclaimed hopefully. \"Don't forget, we carried the body with Dr. Aless' key. If Sedra is Aless, then everything falls into place.\" [2232] \"I'm not so sure,\" Rowe stammered. \"There are actually two possibilities. We're arguing over here because we haven't analyzed it thoroughly enough. The first possibility is that Sedra and Aless are the same person, just like you said, Mr. Bauer. In this case, the body \u2013 the features match both of them \u2013 clears up the mystery of the two missing persons. But if Sedra and Aless are not the same person, then we can only conclude that they are 'physically similar.' We've been avoiding this conclusion because it seems \u2013 uh \u2013 like a clich\u00e9 from a novel, but you can't ignore it.\" [2233] Reen remained silent. [2234] \"Well,\" Bauer stood up, \"maybe these ramblings can solve your problem. I just want to know one thing: who is this corpse? Dr. Aless? Or that damn Britisher Sedra?\" [2235] Two important things happened on Tuesday morning. Chief Sam returned victorious from Ohio, having apprehended the jewel thief who was safely behind bars in an iron window; and the mystery of the \"physically similar\" was solved. [2236] Chapter 26\n[2237] Resurrection\n[2238] The next morning, the two old men and two young people sat under the large oak tree in the peaceful, serene garden of Reine. \"We're here again because...\" the officer cheerfully told Reine, \"Petty told me she and this young man have practically lived here! We have some interesting news to tell you.\" [2239] \"News?\" the old gentleman shrugged, looking tired and weary, then he smiled weakly, a little of his old-time pleasantness creeping into his voice, \"\u2018Fill me with your rich voice, and let all my senses be fed, / Till I am as a seagull come to cormorant's nest on the head.\u2019 (1) I believe the harvest is bountiful.\" [2240] The officer grinned, in a very good mood. \"Judge for yourself!\" he reached into his pocket and pulled out an envelope, \"This morning I received an unexpected telegram from the old man Truncus.\" [2241] The telegram read:\n[2242] Further investigation into Hamnet Sedley reveals an interesting fact. The previous telegram informed you that Hamnet's brother William was missing, and it has now been discovered that William and Hamnet are twin brothers. William has arrived in America, arriving in New York from Bordeaux in late March. He is wanted by the Bordeaux police for illegal entry and malicious destruction of the private library of a French book collector, with the intent to steal rare books. The Frenchman was brutally beaten after discovering that William had damaged the cover of a 1599 edition of Shakespeare's \"The Passionate Pilgrim.\" William's behavior is strange, and he is well-off, like Hamnet, a book collector, and disappeared from Britain three years ago under the pseudonym of Dr. Allis. He served as an expert in rare book auctions, purchasing books for millionaire collectors. His best customer was the recently deceased Sir Humphrey. Both William and Hamnet have no fingerprints on record, and they have no discernible features to consider, but they bear a striking resemblance to each other.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe entire room was devastated during the search, with all furniture overturned, glass items shattered, and wooden wall panels showing signs of being hacked by sharp objects. [1984] Officer Pauling quickly found the weapon, a small axe lying on the floor near the fireplace. [1985] \"That's our axe,\" Maxwell licked his lips again. \"I took it from the kitchen toolbox. I used it to chop wood for the fireplace.\" [1986] \"Is that the only axe here?\" Percy asked. [1987] \"Yes, ma'am.\" [1988] Wooden items and wooden walls had been attacked relentlessly, with wooden fragments scattered everywhere. Even the floor had one spot pryed open, where Maxwell said there had been a carpet. The carpet was now lying in a corner, as if it had been kicked there. Another corner had once held a magnificent Victorian-style floor clock, now lying on the ground, surrounded by glass fragments. The examination revealed that the person wielding the axe had deliberately smashed the cabinet, pulled off the brass pendulum, flipped the clock over, and hacked the back and sides, exposing the delicate mechanism. The clock's hands pointed exactly at twelve. [1989] \"Was this clock still working last night?\" Rowe asked sharply. [1990] \"Yes, sir. I... I was playing solitaire here when the doorbell rang, so I know. The clock was ticking, it was working.\" [1991] \"So he broke this clock in the middle of the night,\" Pechen murmured. \"That might be useful.\" [1992] \"I don't see how,\" Pauling said. \"We know from Maxwell's story that he came at eleven-thirty, didn't he?\" [1993] Jules Renet stood aside, deep in thought, his eyes alert and flashing. [1994] Pechen slowly walked around the room. She checked the desk, with all the drawers pulled out and their contents scattered on the floor, including scattered playing cards. Then she noticed something across the room and squinted. It was a cheap alarm clock sitting on the mantelpiece. [1995] \"Patti, what's wrong?\" Rowley noticed her intense expression. [1996] \"That alarm clock. It's strange to have an alarm clock in the study.\" She walked over to pick it up, and the clock cheerfully ticked away. [1997] \"That's mine, miss.\" Maxwell apologized, seeming to have recovered from the shock, curiously watching the unfolding events. [1998] \"Why do you need a small alarm clock when there's a big grandfather clock in the room?\" Percy suspiciously asked. [1999] \"Oh, about this alarm clock...\" Maxwell hurried over to answer, \"Miss, I've been a bit sick these past few days. On Saturday, I bought some cough medicine in Tarrytown, and the pharmacist told me to take a teaspoonful every four hours. Last night, I took one at eight o'clock, but I'm a bit forgetful, miss. I thought I might forget to take it before going to bed, so I brought the alarm clock in while playing cards, set the alarm for midnight to remind myself to take it first, and then go to sleep. But when I \u2013\"\n[2000] \"So that's it.\" Percy said. The story seemed to have no suspicious aspects, as there was a brown medicine bottle next to the alarm clock on the mantelpiece, about three-quarters full, and a sticky spoon. She checked the alarm clock and found that it was indeed set to twelve o'clock, with the timing rod pushed to the \"on\" position. \"I was thinking\u2026\" she whispered, then looked at her own small watch, which showed the time as eleven fifty-one. \"Gordon, what time is it on your watch?\" [2001] \"About eleven fifty-five.\" [2002] \"Mr. Balling, do you have a watch?\" [2003] \"Eleven fifty-two.\" Balling is unconcerned. \"What's this about?\" [2004] \"I was just wondering how accurate this alarm clock is.\" Percyns smiles slightly, but her eyes are puzzled. \"You see, it's very accurate.\" Indeed, the cheap clock's hands point to eleven fifty-one. [2005] \"Ah, Percyns!\" Laine murmurs, approaching, \"May I take a look?\" He roughly checks it, puts the clock back on the mantelpiece, and returns to his corner. [2006] \"What the hell is that?\" Roeve asked, puzzled, as he wandered around the ruins, flipping through things. He tilted his head back, gazing at the top of a wall. This wall's paneling was different from the others, the bookshelf embedded into the wall from floor to ceiling, while the others only went halfway up. A sliding ladder, similar to those used in shoe stores or libraries, could move along the metal rail track at the bottom of the wall, obviously added by the previous owner to easily reach books that were otherwise out of reach. Above the top shelf, there was a row of cherrywood panels, similar to the ones on the other three walls. They were narrower, seeming like something from an older generation. What caught Roeve's attention was one of the panels, which had been lifted from the wall, acting like a door. [2007] \"Well, looks like a secret cabinet,\" the young man chuckled. \"I hope the next minute the Count of Monte Cristo jumps out of the fireplace.\" He nimbly climbed up the ladder, which was just below the open panel near the ceiling. [2008] \"What in the world is this?\" Bowling grumbled, irritated. \"A secret cabinet! Sounds like something out of a detective novel\u2026 Maxwell, do you know about this?\" [2009] The old man gaped upwards. \"No, sir. I've never seen this before. Oh, this door is quite small.\" [2010] \"Empty!\" Roeve announced with displeasure. \"A perfect place to hide things! Probably\u2014wait\u2014eight inches wide, two inches high, two inches deep\u2026 It must have been done by Dr. Aless. Quite clever. This was recently completed, and the marks from the chisel are still fresh.\" They watched him as he looked around, \"The person who destroyed this place was quite unfortunate. He didn't find this hole.\" Then he pointed to the narrow panel above the top shelf, where the wood had been hacked at viciously with an axe. However, when Roeve closed the small door, there were no marks left. \"What a clever trick! Quite clever. Now, how do we open this thing again?\" [2011] \"Let me take a look, young man,\" Pauling said worriedly. [2012] Roebling reluctantly descended, and the policeman carefully climbed up. As Roebling had said, the secret cabinet was made very skillfully. Now that the door was closed, there were no obvious traces. The gap was very fine, just at the edge of the panel, and it was difficult to notice. Pauling pulled and tugged, but the door remained closed, and the panel's exterior still looked ordinary, although he could hear a hollow sound when he tapped it with his fingers. The frame of the panel was the same as the others, with small wooden roses inlaid. Pauling panted, \"There must be some trick to it.\" He began to touch the small roses, then exclaimed. One of them turned in his fingers. He turned it once, and it didn't work. He turned it again, and the door suddenly jumped open, and he almost fell off the ladder...he removed the door and examined the interior. The primitive spring mechanism was very clever. [2013] \"Alright,\" he descended to the floor. \"Don't worry about this. Whatever was inside...if there was anything, it's already gone. The gap is really small. Let's go upstairs.\" [2014] Dr. Aless's bedroom was hacked apart just like the downstairs study. The bed was completely disassembled, the mattress was torn to shreds, the furniture was shattered, and the floor was lifted up\u2014clearly the person with the axe had continued to search in Dr. Aless's room. There was a small gilded clock in the room. Curiously, it too had escaped the hurricane-like destruction, falling from the table to the floor. The hands stopped at 12:24. [2015] Pecsin's eyes lit up. \"Our friend left a time schedule for his activities. This proves that he first attacked downstairs... Maxwell, does this clock keep good time?\" [2016] \"Yes, miss. All the clocks are accurate, although they're cheap. And I often wind them up to keep them all in sync.\" [2017] \"That's lucky,\" Raine murmured. \"The man is so stupid!\" [2018] \"What?\" Pauling exclaimed sharply. [2019] \"Oh, nothing, Mr. Pauling. I was just criticizing the stupidity of criminals in general.\" [2020] A baritone voice called up the stairs, \"Hey! Look what I found!\" [2021] They rushed down the stairs. A policeman stood in the hallway, holding a flashlight over a dark, dirty corner. Under the light, they saw three shards of glass, one of which was tied with a black long silk ribbon. [2022] Rene picked up the glass shard and brought it into the living room. He put the three shards together, and they formed a perfect circular piece of glass. [2023] \"A single lens,\" he said quietly. [2024] \"Oh my!\" Rowe whispered. [2025] \"A single lens?\" Maxwell blinked. \"Sir, that's quite strange. Dr. Al\u00e9s doesn't wear glasses. I've never seen one in the house, and I\u2014\"\n[2026] \"Dr. Sedra.\" Percy worriedly said. [2027] Chapter 23\n[2028] Symbolic Issue\n[2029] There was nothing to do at the scene. Maxwell accepted the advice and left the house, returning to his peaceful life in Tarrytown, leaving the energetic and hardworking Mr. Bowling to monitor the house, leaving two men to guard the alley and the back of the house - although you could not enter from the back, you would have to flatten the lush shrubs and the fallen leaves piled up high. [2030] Bowling took Maxwell in the police car to Tarrytown. At the invitation of the elderly gentleman, Percy and Rowley drove in the Dromio's car to Hamlet Manor. The young people, grateful for the help of the busy and hardworking Mr. Foster, retreated to their rooms to rest, washed and came downstairs to have a late lunch, although they might not fill their spirits, they could definitely fill their stomachs. During the meal, no one spoke much, Percy was unusually silent, Rowley was full of thoughts, and Rene said some trivial things without mentioning the morning's events. After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincy and retired to the study. [2031] After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincey and retired to the study himself. Percy and Rowe wandered around the spacious Hamlet Manor. They strolled through the charming garden and lay comfortably on the quiet grass. Quincey saw them, chuckled, and disappeared. [2032] Birds chirped, and the grass smelled sweet and fragrant. Both were silent. Rowe turned to look at his companion's face. In the warm sunlight, her cheeks were rosy, and her slender figure was relaxed and graceful. He looked at her warmly, feeling strange; she was both captivating and unattainable. Her eyes were closed, and a fine line appeared between her eyebrows, refusing to joke or flirt. [2033] Rowe sighed. \"Patty, what are you thinking? Please don't frown like that! I like my woman to be ordinary.\" [2034] She murmured, \"Am I frowning?\" Then she opened her eyes and smiled at him. \"Gordon, you're a child. I've been thinking \u2013\"\n[2035] \"I think I have to marry a wife with a sharp mind,\" the young man said lightly. \"The point is, I'm sharp-minded too \u2013 so there would be two of us\u2026\"\n[2036] \"Oh\u2014\" Rowe suddenly lay back on the grass, plucking a blade of grass. [2037] She sat up, her eyes gentle. \"So you figured it out too, Gordon? One of them was the axe-wielder. The state of the house showed that he was looking for something; he didn't know where it was, and he was determined to find it. He was systematically destroying furniture and other things with an axe. Most importantly, this person was not Dr. Ales.\" [2038] Rowe yawned. \"Of course not. If he was Ales, he would have known where to find his own things. The secret cabinet on the wall was definitely his doing.\" [2039] \"Don't be so uninterested, please?\" Percy smiled. \"You're almost tearing your brain apart... I don't know. Your reasoning is correct. The axe-wielder is our unknown variable, and Dr. Ales wouldn't burn down his own place to make firewood, and he knows where the axe-wielder's thing is. But on the other hand, the axe-wielder's thing was found\u2014evidence that the secret cabinet was open, so someone must have opened it.\" [2040] \"So you think there were two people in the house last night? The axe-wielder\u2014such a melodramatic term\u2014why couldn't he find the cabinet himself? He put so much effort into doing something so despicable.\" [2041] \"Alright, you're being clever,\" Percy said. \"You saw it yourself, the cabinet was built so secretly, and Pauling could only open it because he saw that spot and went to feel the rose carving, opening the door. If the door was closed, and the wall was blank, the person looking for something would have to guess the panel, then guess the rose, then know to turn the rose twice \u2013 the odds are a million to one. In other words, that hole couldn't have been accidentally left open! If the ax-wielder knew about the rose and the hole, he wouldn't have gone around chopping everything up. So I say turning the rose lock, opening the cabinet, and taking out what's inside, the person who didn't close the door is not the ax-wielder. If it's not the ax-wielder, then it must be someone else. So we have two people, don't we? Let's see what happens!\" [2042] \"What a female Sherlock Holmes!\" Roe smiled. \"Percy, you're a gem. This reasoning is perfect, and there's a conclusion. The other person \u2013 if there is one \u2013 when did he open the cabinet? That is, was he before or after the ax-wielder?\" [2043] \"Teacher, it must have been after,\" Percy said. \"If this person opened the cabinet first, the ax-wielder would have come later and seen the cabinet door open, and he would have known where the hiding place was immediately. The result would be that he wouldn't have destroyed the house looking for the hiding place... Oh, Gordon, the ax-wielder coming first means he kidnapped Maxwell and kept him in the garage. Then the second person came, and then only heaven knows what happened.\" [2044] They were silent for a long time. Both lay on the grass, looking up at the sky filled with clouds like cotton. Roe's brown hand moved slightly, touching hers. His hand stayed there, and she didn't pull her hand away. [2045] After an early dinner, the three of them came to the British-style old study of Rayne, filled with the smell of leather, books, and wood. Percy sat in the armchair of the old gentleman, picking up a sheet of paper and doodling idly. Rayne and Roe sat at the desk, resting under the dim light of the table lamp. [2046] \"You know,\" Percy suddenly spoke, \"before dinner, I wrote down a few things - hmm, that worried me. I can call them special points.\" [2047] \"Really?\" Ren said softly. \"Child, your persistence is rare among women.\" [2048] \"Sir! That's my main virtue. Should I read my little essay?\" [2049] She took a long piece of paper from her handbag and unfolded it, and began to read aloud clearly, \"One: The person who handed us the sealed envelope with the symbols was Dr. Ales - evidence: his beard and glasses were found in his wardrobe; evidence: he was a 'missing book collector'. Dr. Ales sent Vera to steal the 1599 Jagger from the Saxon family. It was Dr. Ales who sat on the bus rented by the teachers and broke the British Jagger bookcase - Vera's testimony proved this, and the blue cap and gray false mustache found in Dr. Ales' bedroom could further confirm it. But who is Dr. Ales? Is he Hamnet Sedley, as Clara and Vera identified, or is he someone else entirely? Is there any confusion about his identity?\" [2050] \"Two: Who is this Hamnet Sedley? According to Scotland Yard, there is indeed a Hamnet Sedley who was hired as the new curator of the British Museum. But is the person who appeared at the British Museum and claimed to be Sedley really Hamnet Sedley? Or is it, as Dad thought, that someone was pretending to be Hamnet Sedley? He must have something he doesn't want to be known, and he lied about the date he arrived. Did Hamnet Sedley die? Did this person steal his name and take his place? Why did he hide the date of his arrival? What did he do between the actual arrival time and the fabricated arrival time?\" [2051] \"Ah!\" Young Rowe said, \"What a profound thought!\" [2052] Perkins glared at him and continued to read aloud: \"\uff083\uff09If Hamnet Sedley is not Dr. Alessi, what happened to Dr. Alessi? Why did he disappear?\" [2053] \"\uff084\uff09What happened to Donohue?\" [2054] \"\uff085\uff09Who chased me and Gordon and took the envelope?\" [2055] \"\uff086\uff09The axe-wielder is who? He is not Dr. Alessi, but someone else.\" [2056] \"\uff08Seven\uff09 Who followed the axeman and opened the secret cabinet? It could be Dr. Aless himself \u2013 he would know where he hid things.\" [2057] \"Wait a minute, Pechins,\" said Lane. \"How do you know the axeman isn't Dr. Aless? And there were two people in the Aless house last night?\" [2058] \"No, there's one more,\" Pechins frowned. \"The most important and puzzling question.\" She continued, \"\uff08Eight\uff09 What do these points point to? There's no doubt that Dr. Aless's 'million-dollar secret' is involved. But the million-dollar secret is related to the symbol Dr. Aless left for my father, so the solution to everything depends on the last question: what does the symbol mean?\" [2059] After she finished speaking, she put down the paper and began doodling on the table. The two men were silent for a while.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you have any information on the whereabouts of William Sedley or Dr. Allis, please inform the Bordeaux police. Best regards, hunting successfully. [2243] Trunch\n[2244] \"This explains everything, you see,\" Percy exclaimed, \"As twins, Hamnet and William must be as similar as peanuts in a shell, which is why everyone got them confused.\"\" [2245] \"Yes,\" Raine said softly, \"This news is invaluable. Obviously, Sedley is Hamnet Sedley, Dr. Allis is Sedley's brother William, the fugitive wanted by the French police.\" He interlaced his long fingers, \"But the problem still remains the same. Which corpse was discovered? Hamnet or William?\" [2246] Rowe said: \"And William's attempt to steal the 1599 Jagger in Brie. Mr. Reine, have you heard of this Frenchman Pierre Girouard? In fact, I visited him last year.\" - Reine nodded - \"He is the owner of the second rare book. Saxony is the third, and the other one is unknown. Damaged cover, huh? Nonsense, he was looking for the Shakespeare document!\" [2247] \"Understood, boy?\" the sergeant smiled, \"I'm out of this case now. But we're making some progress, aren't we?\" [2248] \"You want to know -\" Percy suddenly spoke, his fingers idly smoothing his clothes, \"who murdered the man in the cellar?\" They were all surprised, and Percy laughed, \"Oh, I don't know the name. It's like solving a geometry problem, a bunch of unknowns. But there's one thing I'm sure of: the killer is the axe-wielder!\" [2249] \"Oh?\" Rowe rolled back onto the grass. [2250] \"From the evidence provided by the grandfather clock, he was in the study at midnight. At 12:24 he was in the bedroom upstairs - the clock in the bedroom is the evidence. The murder took place at 12:26 - just two minutes later! The killer was swinging an axe, and the deep cuts on the victim's wrist and the watch provided evidence. These can prove it.\" [2251] \"I see,\" said Lane, looking up at the blue sky. [2252] \"Isn't that right?\" Peachins asked impatiently. [2253] But Rene did not look at her lips; he seemed to be studying a strange white cloud in the sky. [2254] \"There's one more thing,\" Roe clearly said, \"The single lens glasses we found in the hallway of the house prove that Dr. Sedra was definitely inside. Was he the killer or the victim? We could easily say he was the victim because the body matches his features\u2026\"\n[2255] \"Unless the corpse is Dr. Aleas.\" Peachings said. [2256] \"But who set the bomb?\" asked the sergeant. [2257] Quincy darted ahead of a man in uniform with a brown face. [2258] \"Are you Sergeant Sam?\" asked the stranger. [2259] \"Yes.\" [2260] \"I'm Officer Bolling from the town of Thayer,\" said the stranger. [2261] \"Oh, right. I called him this morning and told him I was back.\" [2262] \"He told me they found a guy on the road between Ewington and Thayer, kind of simple, looked like he was about to starve to death. A hobo, a little crazy. Wouldn't give his name, just babbling about a blue hat.\" [2263] \"Blue hat!\" [2264] \"That's right. They took him to the Thayer hospital. The chief said you should go see him right away.\" [2265] They saw Bowlin pacing in the hospital's waiting room. He shook Sam's hand warmly: \"It's been a while, Sergeant. Damn, things just keep getting worse. Want to see him?\" [2266] \"Of course I do! Who is it?\" [2267] \"I'm not sure. They just finished taking care of him. He's a sturdy old guy, but he's so skinny you can see his ribs, and it's because he's starving.\" [2268] They followed Pauling down the hallway, getting more and more excited. [2269] Pauling opened the door to a private room. A middle-aged man lay motionless on the bed, with a pile of dirty, ragged clothes on the chair beside him. His face was gaunt and lined with deep wrinkles, his beard unkempt, and his eyes fixed on the wall. [2270] Chief Sam's jaw dropped: \"Donoher!\" [2271] \"Is this the missing Irishman?\" Pauling asked anxiously. (Is this the missing Irishman?\") [2272] Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\" (Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\") [2273] \"Hello,\" Sam said cheerfully, walking to the bedside, \"you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\" (Hello, you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\") [2274] Donohue's pale face flushed with a hint of red, and he coughed before speaking. \"Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\" (Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\") [2275] \"Donohue, we've been looking for you since you disappeared. Do you have the strength to talk?\" (Do you have the strength to talk?\") [2276] \"Of course, it's an honor,\" Donohue stroked his scruffy face and spoke with a gradually recovering voice. \"Just as I was visiting the British Museum with the Indiana delegation, I noticed a tall, thin man with a mustache and a peculiar blue hat sneaking out of the museum, with something under his arm - it looked like a book. As a man particularly alert to thieves, I didn't have time to sound the alarm and chased after him. He jumped into a taxi, and I followed. We changed various means of transportation, leaving the city and arriving at a dilapidated wooden house near the main road between Therry and Evenston. After a man in black left the house, I ran to the porch. There was a nameplate under the doorbell that read 'Dr. Allis.' I pressed the doorbell, and the man came to answer the door himself. Although he took off his hat and gray fake mustache, Donohue still recognized him. The mustache was a disguise! Donohue was at a loss because he had no evidence that the man was a thief and might have been just his imagination. However, the mustache was definitely gone... He did not have the authority to arrest him, but he accepted a cordial invitation to enter the house and was taken to the study. Donohue mustered his courage and accused the owner of stealing a book from the museum. [2277] \"He was bold enough,\" Donohue's eyes sparkled, \"to admit his guilt. Then he said he would make up for the loss, pay for it, and so on. I took out my pipe and started smoking, thinking I might as well wait until I had the chance to call the police and have him sent to the nearest police station. But I was too nervous and accidentally dropped my pipe on the ground, startling him. So he asked me to leave, and that was it. I walked down the alley, thinking hard, when suddenly my head was hit hard. That's all I know.\" [2278] When he woke up, he found himself tied up in a dark room with a cloth stuffed in his mouth. He had initially thought that Dr. Allis had followed him and attacked him; he had made this assumption until today. He had discovered that the prison that held him was not Dr. Allis's house but a place he had never seen before. [2279] \"Are you sure?\" the inspector said softly. \"Certainly. Dr. Allis's house is destroyed. Continue, Donohue.\" [2280] \"I don't know how long I was unconscious like a dead pig,\" the Irishman's color returned, looking more comfortable. \"What day is it today? Oh, it doesn't matter. Every day, a man with a gun and a mask comes to feed me once.\" [2281] \"Was it Dr. Aless?\" Percy asked loudly. [2282] \"Miss, I don't know. The lighting was poor, and his voice sounded somewhat like... speaking like an Englishman, yes, that's it. I recognize the accent. I've seen many like him back home. Damn him, he always threatened to torture me, curse him!\" [2283] \"Torture?\" Percy asked in surprise. [2284] \"Miss, it was pretty much that. He talked a lot but never acted. He wanted me to tell him 'where the files were,' \" Donohue chuckled, \"I said, 'You're crazy!' He threatened me even more. I don't know what he meant by 'files,' do you understand?\" [2285] \"Strange,\" Rovery said. [2286] \"There were days he didn't even feed me,\" Donohue complained, \"Damn it, I need a leg of lamb to make up for it!\" He licked his lips and continued with the strange tale. Once \u2013 a long time ago, he said, although he couldn't remember the exact date or time, he couldn't keep track of time anymore \u2013 he heard a sound coming from somewhere in the house, a heavy body being dragged on the ground, obviously someone was thrown into a nearby room. Then he heard someone groaning in pain. After a while, he heard the door slam shut. He wanted to signal his neighbor to communicate, as he believed they were both in trouble, but he was tied up and his mouth was gagged, so all his efforts were in vain. For three days, Donohue hadn't eaten and hadn't seen the person who had captured him. After days of painful struggle, he finally managed to break free and forcefully open the door, finding himself in a dark, dirty, and stinking corridor. He listened carefully, but the house seemed abandoned. He tried to find his fellow sufferer, but all the doors were locked, and there was no response after knocking for a long time. He was very weak himself and feared that the person who had captured him would return, so he slipped out of the house and escaped. [2287] \"Can you still find that place, Donohue?\" Inspector Sam asked angrily. [2288] \"Of course, I can't forget it.\" [2289] \"Wait a minute,\" a young man in a white uniform protested at the door, \"this person is still weak, and I strongly object to him going out.\" [2290] \"Who asked for your approval!\" Donohue yelled, trying to sit up again, only to fall back onto the bed with a \"ouch,\" \"I'm not as strong as before. Doctor, give me some more broth, and I'll be able to guide the rescue team.\" [2291] Donohue sat in the front seat of Reen's car, leading the way, while Bowling and the others sat in another car. They arrived at the spot where he had been discovered by the traffic police earlier. Sergeant Sam helped him out of the car, and the brave Irishman stood blinking in the road, looking around. [2292] \"This way,\" he finally managed to orient himself, and the two men got back in the car. Dromio drove slowly, no more than a hundred yards, when Donohue called out. Dromio turned the car into a narrow lane, less than a mile from Aless's house. [2293] The two cars proceeded cautiously. Three houses passed by the window, disappearing behind the road, and Donohue suddenly shouted, \"Here we are!\" [2294] It was an old, dilapidated, and deserted-looking house, more like a shack than a proper dwelling. There was no sign of life, and the entire place was boarded up, looking as if it had been abandoned for years. [2295] Bowling's men quickly cleared the easy obstacles. An old piece of wood served as a battering ram, and the front door quickly gave way like a rotten nutshell. They rushed into the house, drawing their guns. The house was empty and filthy, with no furniture except for the room where Donohue had been held captive. They kicked open one door after another, finally entering a dark and stinking little room with an iron bed, a washbasin, and a chair. A person was tied up on the bed. [2296] He was unconscious. [2297] Paulin's men carried him out into the sunlight, all staring at the twisted, yellowed face of the victim. The same question appeared in everyone's eyes: Was this the starving, filthy victim, Hamnet or William Sedley? The only certainty was that he was one of the two. [2298] Donohue finished his task, emitting a weak groan and collapsing into the arms of the constable. An ambulance that had been following them sped up and took Donohue away. An intern doctor bent over to examine the unconscious Britisher's feeble limbs. [2299] \"He just fainted. Too tight, lack of food, poor air - ordinary signs of weakness. He'll be fine with care.\" [2300] The newly rescued man had a thin, sunken face with delicate golden stubble on his cheeks. The young doctor administered a nutrient injection, and the man's eyes opened, but his gaze was vacant, and he looked dazedly at the constable's shouted questions before closing his eyes again. [2301] The ambulance drove off, and a car pulled up. A young man without a hat jumped out, later to be identified as a reporter drawn to the scene by the popular mystery rumors. Pauling and Sam were stunned by the questions and answered them all. Despite Rayne's frantic winks and gestures, the information was leaked: all about Dr. Allees, \"the French fugitive being pursued by the police,\" Donohue's harrowing story, the confusion over the identities of the Suddle twins...\n[2302] The young man left with a triumphant smile. Rayne coldly said, \"Constable, that was a mistake.\" [2303] Sam blushed. At that moment, a man walked up to Pauling and reported that despite a thorough search, there was no evidence linking the kidnapper to the house. \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2304] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2305] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2306] The two teams sat silently in their own cars for ten minutes before Gordon Rowe finally said, \"Let's talk about the mystery.\" [2307] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[2308] (1) From Shakespeare's play \"Antony and Cleopatra\"\n[2309] Chapter 27\n[2310] Three hundred years of crime\n[2311] Inspector Sam's face was grim. \"The first thing we need to figure out is \u2013 who is he?\" [2312] They arrived at the Teri Town Hospital the next morning, gathering around the Britisher's bed. The chief doctor had called earlier to say that the patient could speak, and careful care, sedatives, and a good night's sleep had worked wonders on him. He had shaved, and there was a glint of animation in his dull face, and his eyes were distant and intelligent. They saw him sitting up in bed, with morning papers spread out on the quilt, cheerfully chatting with the man in the next bed. [2313] The Britisher raised his pale eyebrows. \"What's the question? I don't understand your meaning.\" He looked them over shrewdly, one by one, as if weighing them with a secret scale. His voice was weak, but familiar in tone. [2314] \"I am Dr. Hamnett Sedra.\" [2315] \"Ah!\" said Lane, \"That's great news for Joate!\" [2316] \"Dr. Jotter? Oh, right, Dr. Jotter! He must be worried. Donohue thought I was the man he was tracking, the man in the blue cap. Ha, ha! We look so alike - it's amazing.\u201d He composed himself, \"You know, he's my twin brother.\" [2317] \"So you know he's dead?\" Percy exclaimed. Reen glanced at the detective, who was turning red. [2318] \"I've been surrounded by reporters all morning. Then these newspapers told me everything. Based on the forensic description of the body, it must have been my brother William. You know, he always used a pseudonym, Dr. Alessi.\" [2319] \"Well,\" Sam said, \"Listen, Dr. Sedlar, it seems the case is finally solved. But what the answer is, I know for sure. We've told you - you've got some things that don't add up, and now it's about your brother. We need to know the truth. Since your brother is dead, there's no need to keep it to yourself.\" [2320] Dr. Sedlar sighed. \"I suppose you're right! Well, I'll tell you everything.\" He closed his eyes, his voice barely audible, \"You've made a big fuss about you not telling us when you arrived in New York. I secretly came first to correct a disgraceful behavior of my brother William.\" He paused, and no one spoke; he opened his eyes, \"There are too many people here.\" He suddenly said. [2321] \"Oh, come on, doctor,\" Rowe said, \"we're all in this together, and as for Donohue\u2026\"\n[2322] \"I'm deaf, dumb, and blind,\" the Irishman said with a smile. [2323] He told the story reluctantly.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."